  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationalale:\n   - We are aiming to improve the Erd\u0151s constant upper bound from 0.381415 to approximately 0.380876.\n   - The previous approach used SLSQP with 280 points and a constant initial function (0.5). This needed improvement due to potential local optima.\n   - The new approach will use multi-scale optimization on different point resolutions (280, 560) to explore both coarser and finer representations.\n   - Key changes include: piecewise linear interpolation during optimization, advanced local search (Nelder-Mead + SLSQP), global exploration with random restarts, and Nelder-Mead local searches before SLSQP.\n   - We'll use a budget-conscious scheme: first optimize the fixed 280-point structure, then upgrade to 560 points for refined results.\n   - If upgrades exceed time, we restore feasibility from the parent solution.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   - Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k integral |h(x)(1-h(x+k))| dx\n   - Constraints: 0\u2264h(i)\u22641\u2200i, sum(h(i)/dx)=1\u2192sum(h)=n_points/2\n   - Representations: \n        Base: n=280 (given parent)\n        Refined: n=560 (doubled parent plus normalization)\n   - Duality: Use Nelder-Mead for simplex-based search, then SLSQP for constraint handling.\n   - Evaluation: Compute correlation via `np.correlate(mode='full')*dx` to match the original score.\n   - Initialization: Use provided parent, then random perturbations; repair constraint by scaling and clipping.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   - Validate input `initial_h_values` for n=280\n   - Multi-point resolutions: [280, 560]\n   - Upgrade resolution by: h_560 = np.tile(initial_h, 2) * 0.5\n   - For each resolution:\n        a. Define dx=2.0/n_points, integral_target = n_points/2\n        b. Define objective(h): clip h\u2192[0,1], correlate with (1-h), return max*dx\n        c. Define constraint: sum(h) - integral_target\n        d. Multi-start optimization:\n            - Random seed initialization by: random shift, scaling, noise\n            - Repair by: min_step=0.05 adjustments to meet slope-based constraints\n        e. Use Nelder-Mead (with fixing) followed by constrained SLSQP\n        f. Return best candidate across (C5, h) with finite score\n   - Feasibility restoration: Use parent fallback if repair fails\n   - Budget control: Time budget checked per resolution block\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   - Primary: 280 base + 560 refinement\n   - Noise: Initial noise std=0.03 for 280; 0.01 for 560\n   - Local search: Nelder-Mead (300 steps) then SLSQP (200 iter)\n   - Repair mode: Switch to Nelder-Mead repair if constraint fails\n   - Budget: 50s for 280 base; 60s for 560 upgrade\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   - Main cost: Objective eval (O(n^2) correlations). n=560 doubles time.\n   - Feasibility: Ensure every candidate satisfies budget check before eval\n   - Restore: Precomputed parent fallback (140, 280, 560 points)\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Return (h_array, C5_score, n_points) in (6,) shape\n   - Validate: sum(h)/dx \u22481, 0\u2264h\u22641, obj is finite\n   - Best at termination: Minimum C5 found; output all three arrays\n   - Dependencies: Use scipy.optimize.minimize with 'SLSQP' and 'Nelder-Mead'\n\nThe algorithm implements this with:\n   - Tiered resolution scaling\n   - Probabilistic repair methods\n   - Local-global search combinations\n   - Strict budget enforcement",
